      en: "Mount Phousi is a roughly 100-metre-high hill in the heart of Luang Prabang, rising on a peninsula at the confluence of the Mekong and Nam Khan rivers. Due to its central and elevated location, it is the city's most popular vantage point, especially in the late afternoon when hundreds of visitors gather to watch the spectacular sunset over the Mekong and the surrounding mountains. The hill is also an important spiritual site, housing several Buddhist shrines along the winding ascent paths. At the summit sits the Wat Chom Si temple complex, whose distinctive golden stupa was built in 1804 and is visible from far away."
